# .gitignore for the Idira vibe-coding workshop
#
# Two audiences produce junk in this repo:
#   1. The workshop owner, building and dry-running the material.
#   2. Sixty attendees, who each clone it and then run the lab inside it.
#
# The second one matters more than usual. Attendees create virtual environments,
# scratch projects and copied skills inside the folder they cloned — none of that
# belongs here.
#
# ⚠️ READ THE LAST SECTION BEFORE ADDING A SECRET PATTERN. The fake credentials in
# sandbox-app/ are the point of this workshop and must stay tracked.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Python virtual environments  (prework step 3)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The lab standardises on `.venv` in the repo root. The rest are here because
# people who already know Python will use their own habit instead.

.venv/
.venv-*/
venv/
env/
ENV/
.python-version
pyvenv.cfg


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Python build and cache artefacts
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
*.so
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.coverage
.coverage.*
htmlcov/
*.egg-info/
build/
dist/
pip-log.txt
pip-wheel-metadata/


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Agent working files
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Machine-local Claude Code settings and session state. `.claude/settings.json`
# (no `.local`) is shared config and IS tracked if you ever add one.

.claude/settings.local.json
.claude/*.local.json
.claude/projects/
.claude/todos/
.claude/shell-snapshots/
.claude/statsig/
.claude/history.jsonl

# Lessons 3 and 5 tell attendees to copy skills into the app they are working
# in. Those are copies of skills/ — the originals are tracked, the copies are not.
sandbox-app/.claude/


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Downloaded tools and built distributions
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The idsec CLI is a downloaded binary (prework step 5). Never commit it —
# attendees fetch the build for their own platform from the releases page.
#
# These are anchored with a leading slash on purpose. Unanchored, `idsec` also
# matches skills/idsec/ — the tracked SKILL.md that teaches the CLI — and the
# lab loses a skill it hands to every attendee.

/idsec
/idsec.exe
/idsec/
*.tar.gz
*.tgz
*.zip

# The attendee-facing bundle, if you build it in place (owner-prep gate G3).
vibe-coding-workshop-dist/


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Attendee scratch space
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Lessons 6 and 8 start from an empty folder. If someone creates it inside their
# clone rather than beside it, these names catch the common cases. Not
# exhaustive — it cannot be — but it covers the folder names the lab suggests.

scratch/
playground/
my-first-app/
my-app/
my-project/
from-nothing/


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Operating system and editor noise
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# macOS
.DS_Store
.AppleDouble
._*
.Spotlight-V100
.Trashes

# Windows
Thumbs.db
Thumbs.db:encryptable
ehthumbs.db
Desktop.ini
$RECYCLE.BIN/
*.lnk

# Editors
.vscode/
!.vscode/extensions.json
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.\#*
\#*\#


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Real credentials — belt and braces
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Nothing in this repo needs any of these. They are here because an agent asked
# to "put the credentials somewhere safer" will sometimes reach for a .env file,
# and a room of sixty people running that prompt is exactly when you want this
# net in place. See the helper runbook, Lesson 4.

.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
!**/*.key.example
.aws/
credentials
.idsec/
.netrc


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 🚨 DO NOT IGNORE THESE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The whole workshop depends on the planted fake credentials being present in a
# fresh clone. Lesson 2 asks an agent to find them; Lesson 4 deletes one of them;
# Part 1's Stage 2 asks the attendee to find them by hand first. If a future
# secret-scanning pattern hides these files, those lessons have no content.
#
# Every value is a public example placeholder — AWS's own documentation strings
# and obviously-fake GitHub, Stripe, Slack and Postgres values. None of them
# authenticate to anything, anywhere. See the note at the end of README.md.
#
# ⚠️ ai-harness-app/sandbox/.env is matched by the `.env` rule above, and Stage 2
# specifically teaches that a hidden dotfile is the secret people miss. Without
# this negation a fresh clone is missing two of the six planted secrets and the
# lesson quietly loses its punchline.
#
# These negations are load-bearing. Leave them last so nothing added above can
# override them.

!sandbox-app/config/
!sandbox-app/config/settings.py
!sandbox-app/config/integrations.json
!sandbox-app/config/__init__.py
!ai-harness-app/sandbox/.env
